## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

---

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` to compare props. Primitives compare by value; objects and functions compare by reference.

Every render creates **new references** for inline objects and functions, so even if the data is identical, the child sees a "changed" prop.

```jsx
// Bad: new object reference on every parent render
<Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} />

// Bad: new function reference on every parent render
<Child onClick={() => handleClick(id)} />
```

This is the root cause of most unnecessary re-renders.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component. Skips re-render if all props pass a shallow equality check.
- Use when: a child is expensive to render and receives stable props.
- Don't use when: props change almost every render anyway — the comparison cost isn't free.

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a function reference across renders.
- Use when: passing a callback to a `memo`-wrapped child, or as a dependency of `useEffect`.
- Not useful without `React.memo` on the receiver — the child re-renders regardless.

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed value.
- Use when: computation is expensive (sorting large arrays, heavy transformations).
- Don't use for cheap computations — the overhead exceeds the savings.

Rule of thumb: **`React.memo` + `useCallback`/`useMemo` are a package deal**. One without the other is usually pointless.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer re-renders whenever the context value changes by reference — even if the consumer only uses part of the value.

```jsx
// Bad: new object on every render that wraps Provider
function App() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    // This object literal is recreated every render
    <AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      <Tree />
    </AuthContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Fixes:
- **`useMemo`** the context value.
- **Split contexts** — separate frequently-changing state from stable values so consumers only subscribe to what they need.
- Put the `Provider` high enough that its parent rarely re-renders.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ProductList` re-renders on every `App` render, even when `products` hasn't changed:

```jsx
function App() {
  const [cart, setCart] = useState([]);

  // New function reference every render
  const handleAddToCart = (item) => {
    setCart(prev => [...prev, item]);
  };

  return (
    <>
      <CartSummary cart={cart} />
      {/* Re-renders every time cart changes, even though products didn't */}
      <ProductList products={products} onAdd={handleAddToCart} />
    </>
  );
}

function ProductList({ products, onAdd }) {
  console.log('ProductList rendered');
  return products.map(p => <Product key={p.id} product={p} onAdd={onAdd} />);
}
```

**After** — `ProductList` only re-renders when `products` changes:

```jsx
function App() {
  const [cart, setCart] = useState([]);

  // Stable reference — only recreated if dependencies change
  const handleAddToCart = useCallback((item) => {
    setCart(prev => [...prev, item]);
  }, []); // no deps: setCart is stable from useState

  return (
    <>
      <CartSummary cart={cart} />
      <ProductList products={products} onAdd={handleAddToCart} />
    </>
  );
}

// Skips re-render if products and onAdd references are the same
const ProductList = React.memo(function ProductList({ products, onAdd }) {
  console.log('ProductList rendered');
  return products.map(p => <Product key={p.id} product={p} onAdd={onAdd} />);
});
```

Key changes: `useCallback` stabilizes the function reference; `React.memo` makes `ProductList` check whether to bail out. Neither alone would work.

---

**Practical heuristic**: profile first (React DevTools Profiler), optimize second. Premature memoization adds complexity without measurable benefit in most cases.
